WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:03.510
Once we are happy with our
trained machine learning model,

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.150
how can we serve our
predictions at scale?

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:11.340
Find out on this episode
of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.930
Google's Cloud Machine
Learning Engine

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:15.300
enables you to
create a prediction

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.780
service for your TensorFlow
model without any ops work.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.510
Get more time with your
data by going from a trained

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:24.180
model to a deployed
auto scaling prediction

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.460
service in a matter of minutes.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:30.210
So we've gathered our
data, and finally finished

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.670
training up a suitable
model and validating

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.140
that it performs well.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.960
So now we're ready to
move to the second phase--

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.060
serving those predictions.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.400
When taking on the challenge
of serving predictions,

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.560
we would ideally want
to deploy a model that

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.630
is purpose-built for serving.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.480
In particular, a fast,
lightweight model

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.340
that is static
since we don't need

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.590
to do any updating while
serving predictions.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.980
Additionally, if we want
our prediction server

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.790
to scale with demand, that adds
another layer of complexity

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:01.000
to the problem.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.640
It turns out that
TensorFlow already

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.440
has a built-in
function that can take

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:08.220
care of generating an optimized
model for serving predictions.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.110
It handles all the
adjustments needed,

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.419
which saves you a lot of work.

00:01:11.419 --> 00:01:12.960
The function that
we're interested in

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.180
is called
export_savedmodel, and we

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.970
can run it directly from
our classifier object

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.356
once we're satisfied with the
state of the trained model.

00:01:21.356 --> 00:01:22.980
This will take a
snapshot of your model

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.400
and export it as a set of files
that you can use elsewhere.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.410
Over time, as your
model improves,

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.290
you can continue to produce
exported models in order

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.390
to provide multiple versions.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:36.500
These exported files are made
up of a file and a folder.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.570
The file is called
saved_model.pb,

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:41.700
which defines the
model structure.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.130
The variables folder
holds two files,

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:47.160
supplying the trained
weights in our model.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.080
Once you have an
exported model, you're

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.150
ready to serve it in production.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.820
Here are your two
primary options--

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:57.150
you can use TensorFlow serving
or the Cloud Machine Learning

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.730
Engine prediction service.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:00.690
TensorFlow serving is
a part of TensorFlow,

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.370
and available on GitHub.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.140
However, today we will
focus our attention

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.840
on Cloud Machine Learning
Engine's prediction service,

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.570
though they both have very
similar file interfaces.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:11.070
Cloud Machine
Learning Engine allows

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.950
you to take this exported model
and turn it into a prediction

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:17.160
service with a built-in
endpoint and auto-scaling which

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.230
goes all the way down to zero.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.510
It's also complete with a
feature-rich command line

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.770
tool, API, and UI.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:26.110
So we can interact with
it in a number of ways,

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:27.535
depending on our preferences.

00:02:27.535 --> 00:02:29.910
Let's see an example of how
to use Cloud Machine Learning

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:33.210
Engine's prediction service
with our Iris sample.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.730
First, we will run
export_savedmodel

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.740
on our trained classifier.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.330
This will generate
an exported model

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.580
that we can use for
our prediction service.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:45.644
Next, we'll upload those
files to Google Cloud storage.

00:02:45.644 --> 00:02:48.060
Cloud Machine Learning Engine
will read from Cloud storage

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.250
when creating a
new model version.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.380
Be sure to choose the
regional storage class,

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.490
and to ensure that your compute
and storage in the same region.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:59.020
In the Cloud Machine
Learning Engine UI,

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:02.460
we can create a new model,
which is really just the wrapper

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.950
for our released versions.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.370
Versions hold our
individual exported models

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:09.622
while the model
abstraction helps route

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:11.580
the incoming traffic to
the appropriate version

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:12.870
of your choice.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.300
Here we are in the
models list view

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:18.270
where we can create a new model
All it takes to create a model

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.170
is to just give it a name.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.410
Let's call it ours Iris Model.

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:24.614
Next, we'll create a
version by choosing

00:03:24.614 --> 00:03:26.280
a name for this
particular model version

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.890
and pointing it to our
Cloud storage directory that

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:30.810
holds our exported files.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.780
And just like that,
we've created our model.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.960
All it took was pointing the
service at our exported model

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.850
and giving it a name.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:40.500
How could it take
so little work?

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.530
Well, the service handled
all the operational aspects

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.900
of setting up and
securing the endpoint.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.630
Moreover, we didn't need to
write our own code for scaling

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.490
it out based on demand.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:53.460
And since this is the
Cloud, this elasticity

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:56.280
means you don't need to
pay for unused compute

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:57.810
when demand is low.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:00.240
By setting up a prediction
service for our Iris Model

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.430
that didn't need
any ops work, we've

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:04.530
been able to go
from trained model

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:06.810
to deployed auto-scaling
prediction service

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:10.920
in a matter of minutes, which
means more time to get back

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.850
to working with your data.

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:15.690
Thanks for watching this
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.300
Be sure to subscribe to the
channel to catch all the latest

